For testing only: the API is reachable but the family selector returns an empty list. How should the UI distinguish this from loading or an API error?

UI states for the family selector

State	What the UI should show	How to determine it
Loading	A spinner, skeleton rows, or “Loading families…” text.	Set while the request is in flight (before any response is received).
Success – empty list	A clear, friendly message such as “No families found. You can create a new family or contact support.” plus the selector UI (e.g., an empty dropdown or a placeholder).	Received HTTP 200 (or 2xx) with a payload that contains an empty array ([] or { families: [] }).
API error	An error banner/message like “Unable to load families. Please try again later.” with a Retry button. Optionally show the specific error code/message if helpful for debugging.	Received a non‑2xx status (e.g., 500, 503, timeout) or a network exception.
Implementation sketch (pseudo‑code)
const [state, setState] = useState<'idle' | 'loading' | 'success' | 'error'>('idle');
const [families, setFamilies] = useState<Family[]>([]);
const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);

useEffect(() => {
  setState('loading');
  fetch('/api/v1/families')
    .then(r => {
      if (!r.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${r.status}`);
      return r.json();
    })
    .then(data => {
      setFamilies(data.families ?? []);   // <-- may be []
      setState('success');
      setError(null);
    })
    .catch(err => {
      setError(err.message);
      setState('error');
    });
}, []);

return (
  <>
    {state === 'loading' && <Spinner label="Loading families…" />}
    {state === 'error' && (
      <Alert type="danger">
        Unable to load families. {error && <small> ({error})</small>}
        <Button onClick={retry}>Retry</Button>
      </Alert>
    )}
    {state === 'success' && (
      <>
        {families.length === 0 ? (
          <Info>
            No families found. You can <Link to="/create-family">create one</Link>
            or contact support.
          </Info>
        ) : (
          <FamilySelect options={families} />
        )}
      </>
    )}
  </>
);

Why this distinction matters
Loading tells the user to wait; they know the app is working.
Empty list is a legitimate result—there simply aren’t any families yet—so the UI offers a constructive next step (create a family, invite, etc.).
Error signals something went wrong; the user gets a clear message and a way to retry, preventing confusion with the “no families” case.

By separating these three conditions with distinct visual cues and messaging, the UI gives the user accurate feedback about what’s happening behind the scenes.

